.TH WUW 1 "October 2026" "wuw 1.0.11" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wuw \- show what is happening with a runit or SysV service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wuw
.RB [ \-n " lines" ]
.I service
.RB [ l ]
.br
.B wuw \-\-list-services
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wuw
prints a compact service status containing its enabled and active state,
process tree, resource use, and recent readable log messages.
It uses runit when a service directory is present and otherwise falls back to
the service's SysV init script.
.PP
Service status requires root privileges. When a requested service exists and
.B wuw
is invoked by another user, it re-executes its absolute path through
.BR sudo (8),
preserving the service name, log action and
.B \-n
option. Service listing and shell completion do not invoke
.BR sudo .
.PP
Append
.B l
to browse a per-service log in
.BR less (1)
instead of printing status. The same automatic privilege elevation applies.
The current log opens at its end. Completed svlogd rotations
.RI ( @*.s )
are available in chronological order: type
.B :p
for an older file and
.B :n
for a newer one. Scrolling stays within the selected file.
Press
.B Shift+F
to follow new entries,
.B Ctrl+C
to stop following, and
.B q
to quit. Following reopens the current file after rotation.
.PP
Files are opened directly without concatenating the history or counting
all lines. Shared syslog files are not scanned in this mode, and timestamps
are displayed as stored. The pager runs in secure mode with shell/editor
commands and input preprocessors disabled.
.SH SERVICE DESCRIPTIONS
The description after the service name in the status header is read from
local files in this order:
.IP 1. 4
The runit service's run script: # Short-Description:, then # Description:.
.IP 2. 4
/etc/init.d/SERVICE: # Short-Description:, then # Description:.
.IP 3. 4
Description= in SERVICE.service, checking /lib/systemd/system,
/usr/lib/systemd/system, then /etc/systemd/system. These files, when present,
are read as text metadata.
.IP 4. 4
The literal text service if no description is found.
.PP
For runit, the script is read from the service directory shown in Loaded,
following the supervised service link. For example, when nginx's Loaded path
is /usr/share/runit/sv.now/nginx, its run script is
/usr/share/runit/sv.now/nginx/run.
.PP
To give a runit service a description, add a comment near the top of its
run script:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/sh
# Short-Description: SSHGuard Server
.fi
.PP
Use a single-line description. Only the first matching line for each field
is considered; an empty value advances to the next field or source.
.SH RUNIT STATE
The Active line reports the current state and Wanted reports the requested
state. A red blinking dot means the service is down and wants to start.
A green blinking dot means it is stopping or restarting. Only the dot blinks;
text stays steady and names the transition even without colours.
ANSI blink support in the terminal is required to see the animation.
.PP
An expected down state is grey, including a completed one-time run.
A running one-time service stays green and reports Wanted: down (no restart).
Runit status does not retain the exit code, so
an expected down state does not certify successful completion.
The service's state, PID and age are read independently of its logger.
.PP
Running services show the start time and a readable age (e.g. 13 days ago)
calculated from the runtime reported by runit. While stopping or restarting,
the timestamp is labelled "; running since ..." to distinguish the process
start from the start of the transition.
Down and finishing times measure the age of the current runit state.
Runit does not expose the time of the start/stop request; the displayed runtime
is not the time spent waiting for shutdown.
Running means a process is executing; it does not certify readiness.
.SH SYSV STATE
The enabled state includes boot tasks registered in /etc/rcS.d.
A successful status command without an identifiable PID is displayed as
active (no PID found), followed by the script's status message.
This can mean configuration is active, as with resolvconf; it does not
establish that a daemon is running or that a one-time task completed
successfully.
.SH PROCESS GROUPS
The Group path identifies the service and its supervisor backend, for example
/runit/sshguard or /sysv/mysql. Processes sharing the main process's parent's
process group are listed directly beneath it. Separate groups appear as
labelled branches, for example 1766 /bin/sh /usr/sbin/sshguard, containing their
remaining service processes. The branch represents the group leader once;
it is not repeated among the members. The leading number is the actual process group ID (PGID),
read from /proc/PID/stat.
The label is the group leader's full command line, including the script path
when run through an interpreter. If the leader has exited, a surviving
member's command line is used, falling back to its process name or the service
name if unavailable. These are process
groups, not cgroups. Only the main process and its descendants are listed;
the runit supervisor and dedicated logger are excluded.
Processes with unreadable PGIDs are listed directly under the service.
If the parent's PGID cannot be read, the main process's group is used as the
default instead.
.SH MEMORY ACCOUNTING
Memory is estimated by summing proportional set size (PSS) and page-table
memory for the main process and its descendants. This avoids counting shared
mappings once for every process. If PSS cannot be read for every process, WUW
falls back to summed resident set size for the complete process tree. The
output is labelled
.B (estimated)
or
.B (RSS)
accordingly. Neither method includes all memory tracked by a dedicated cgroup.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
Show at most this many recent log lines in status output. The default is 12.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Display a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-list-services
List discoverable runit and SysV service names, one per line. This interface is
used by the packaged shell completions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WUW_COLOR
Set to
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour in automatic mode when set to a non-empty value.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the service is active, 3 when it is inactive or its state is
unknown, 4 when no service definition can be found, and 2 for invalid usage.
Log browsing returns the pager's exit status, or 1 when no readable per-service
log or pager is available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/service
Enabled runit services.
.TP
.I /etc/sv
Runit service definitions.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d
SysV init scripts.
.TP
.I /var/log/runit
Common location for per-service runit logs.
